Guard deployments against the wrong deployer.

A Kolla Ansible deployer image packages one version of the deployment
tool and is built alongside one set of service images. Nothing stops an
operator from running it against a configuration naming other images,
against a deployment a newer deployer has already changed, or as an
upgrade across more releases than upstream supports. Each of these
fails late and messily, if it fails at all.

This change adds checks which run first in every playbook except
destroy.yml, which must work whatever the versions so that a mistake
can always be cleaned up.

* Every successful deploy, deploy-containers, reconfigure, upgrade and
  migrate-container-engine writes a record of what changed each host to
  /etc/kolla/kolla-ansible-deployment.json on that host: the release,
  the Kolla Ansible version and git commit, the tag, the action and the
  time. It is owned by config_owner_user and config_owner_group with
  mode 0600, like other configuration. It is written whatever tags were
  given, because a partial deploy has still changed the host.
* When /etc/kolla-ansible/deployer.json exists, that is when Kolla
  Ansible runs from a deployer image, the new deployer-checks.yml play
  refuses an openstack_tag or openstack_release which differs from the
  image's, and compares the image with each target's record. An older
  release, or the same release with an older Kolla Ansible version, is
  refused. A newer release may run only bootstrap-servers, pull,
  prechecks, gather-facts and upgrade, and upgrade may move only to the
  next release, or from a .1 release to the next .1. An upgrade to
  master is allowed with a warning.
* A virtualenv install cannot tell which release it is for, because
  openstack_release is set by the operator, so it writes the record but
  does not check it.
* The new --override-deployer-checks option turns each refusal into a
  warning, for example after an upgrade done by hand.
* The rules are in a filter plugin, so that they are unit tested.
  gather-facts.yml imports the play, so that it runs once for every
  playbook which gathers facts. Playbooks which act on the deploy host
  alone check the configuration only.

